    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d527">

                <pb facs="0652" n="538" sameAs="frus:frus1924v01/pg538"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.113/793</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Sweden</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gittings</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Stockholm</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-12-01"><hi rendition="#italic">December 1,
                        1924</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received December 17.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 319</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to this Legation’s despatch
                    No. 272 of September 10, 1924,<anchor n="68" xml:id="d527-f1"/> and previous
                    correspondence concerning the proposed modification of the terms of the existing
                    embargo on the shipment of arms and munitions of war to China (as set forth in
                    the Department’s unnumbered instruction of October 17, 1923<anchor n="69"
                        xml:id="d527-f2"/>), I have the honor to transmit, as enclosures, in the
                    original French text and in translation, the reply of the Royal Swedish Ministry
                    for Foreign Affairs, dated November 26, 1924.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Sterett Gittings</hi>,
                    Jr.</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d527-a1.xml" xpointer="d527-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
